WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:16.075
This is the database lecture for KB8029, Bioinformatics, given by Petras Kundrotas of the Center for Computational Biology at the University of Kansas and the Department of Biochemistry and Biophysics here at Stockholm University.

2
00:00:16.075 --> 00:00:19.750
It runs in three parts of about forty-five minutes.

3
00:00:19.750 --> 00:00:28.225
The first is about what biological databases are and how records at the DNA, RNA and protein levels connect to one another.

4
00:00:28.225 --> 00:00:35.300
The second is about finding a record from a name or an identifier, and about reading what an identifier tells you.

5
00:00:35.300 --> 00:00:41.475
The third is about retrieving information through genome browsers and about searching the literature.

6
00:00:42.075 --> 00:00:45.450
This lecture joins three practical questions.

7
00:00:45.450 --> 00:00:47.925
Which database should you use?

8
00:00:47.925 --> 00:00:50.675
How do you get the information out of it?

9
00:00:50.675 --> 00:00:54.625
And how do you find and judge the literature that supports it?

10
00:00:54.625 --> 00:01:03.525
The first part is about the databases themselves, and about how records for genomic DNA, transcripts and proteins relate to one another.

11
00:01:03.525 --> 00:01:13.425
Human beta-globin runs through the whole lecture as the worked example: the same biological question leads to different records depending on what you actually need.

12
00:01:13.425 --> 00:01:18.550
Identifiers, cross-references and evidence are what let you tell which is which.

13
00:01:18.550 --> 00:01:26.100
The second part is about getting the data out — the command line against a database's own web pages and genome browsers.

14
00:01:26.100 --> 00:01:41.950
Browsers like UCSC and Ensembl put genomic, transcript and protein information in one interface, so you can start from a gene, look at its DNA, choose a transcript, and take both its sequence and the protein, without visiting a different website for each level.

15
00:01:41.950 --> 00:01:50.750
The third part is about the literature: bibliographic databases, scholarly search engines, and the AI tools that now sit alongside them.

16
00:01:50.750 --> 00:01:57.425
PubMed is the worked example — building a query, checking how it was interpreted, and finding the full text.

17
00:01:57.425 --> 00:02:02.925
AI can suggest terms and related papers and help you form a first overview.

18
00:02:02.925 --> 00:02:06.350
What it cannot do is stand in for checking the source.

19
00:02:06.950 --> 00:02:16.350
A database is an organised collection of data, and the organisation is the point: it lets you search by identifier, by organism, by sequence.

20
00:02:16.350 --> 00:02:24.650
Underneath there is software controlling how data are stored, updated and retrieved, and hardware providing the storage and the computing.

21
00:02:24.650 --> 00:02:30.525
In biology we usually use one name for the whole service — the data and the tools together.

22
00:02:30.525 --> 00:02:37.800
Biological databases present their contents as records: for a gene, a transcript, a protein, a structure.

23
00:02:37.800 --> 00:02:43.925
A record may hold a sequence, annotations, links to related records, and references.

24
00:02:43.925 --> 00:02:52.375
And different claims inside one record can rest on quite different kinds of evidence, which is worth remembering before you quote any of them.

25
00:02:52.375 --> 00:02:56.725
Human beta-globin, HBB, is our example throughout.

26
00:02:56.725 --> 00:03:04.850
Searching for it can land you on a gene record, a transcript record or a protein record, depending on where you search and what you ask.

27
00:03:05.450 --> 00:03:10.800
Bioinformatics used to begin by downloading a database and searching it locally.

28
00:03:10.800 --> 00:03:13.975
For some resources that is still sensible.

29
00:03:13.975 --> 00:03:21.125
For the largest archives it has become impractical for most laboratories — and storage is only part of the problem.

30
00:03:21.125 --> 00:03:27.050
Downloads, updates, indexes, backups and the software to search it all have to be maintained.

31
00:03:27.050 --> 00:03:32.250
The table uses decimal units, so each step multiplies by a thousand.

32
00:03:32.250 --> 00:03:35.200
A terabyte is a thousand gigabytes.

33
00:03:35.200 --> 00:03:39.400
The biological numbers are dated snapshots, which matters.

34
00:03:39.400 --> 00:03:51.425
In the March twenty twenty-six UniProt release, the compressed Swiss-Prot text is about seven hundred megabytes; TrEMBL, which is the unreviewed part, is about a hundred and twenty-one gigabytes.

35
00:03:51.425 --> 00:03:59.700
The Protein Data Bank core archive, snapshotted on the first of January twenty twenty-six, was about one point six terabytes.

36
00:03:59.700 --> 00:04:07.550
Which is the interesting point: one and a half terabytes fits on an ordinary portable hard drive costing a couple of thousand kronor.

37
00:04:07.550 --> 00:04:10.250
The storage is not what stops you.

38
00:04:10.250 --> 00:04:12.275
The maintenance is.

39
00:04:12.875 --> 00:04:16.175
So who does maintain the complete collections?

40
00:04:16.175 --> 00:04:23.350
A laboratory can hold local copies, and a fixed snapshot is genuinely useful for reproducible analysis.

41
00:04:23.350 --> 00:04:37.275
But keeping the largest archives current and available to everyone needs shared public infrastructure, and the International Nucleotide Sequence Database Collaboration — INSDC — is how that is coordinated across countries.

42
00:04:37.275 --> 00:04:39.375
Three institutions.

43
00:04:39.375 --> 00:04:45.625
In Bethesda, Maryland, the National Center for Biotechnology Information runs GenBank.

44
00:04:45.625 --> 00:04:52.800
In Hinxton, near Cambridge, the European Bioinformatics Institute runs the European Nucleotide Archive.

45
00:04:52.800 --> 00:04:59.175
In Mishima, Japan, the National Institute of Genetics runs the DNA Data Bank of Japan.

46
00:04:59.175 --> 00:05:03.100
Note that the institution and the archive are not the same thing.

47
00:05:03.100 --> 00:05:07.025
NCBI provides a great deal besides GenBank.

48
00:05:07.025 --> 00:05:20.650
Between them they preserve sequence information at several stages: raw reads, assembled sequences, and the annotations describing features within them, along with metadata about the biological source and the experiment.

49
00:05:20.650 --> 00:05:28.900
That metadata is what makes a sequence useful — a sequence without knowing which organism it came from, and how, is worth much less.

50
00:05:28.900 --> 00:05:31.275
You submit to one partner.

51
00:05:31.275 --> 00:05:33.275
Not to all three.

52
00:05:33.875 --> 00:05:38.650
The three partners offer different interfaces to the same shared records.

53
00:05:38.650 --> 00:05:45.000
The pages differ; the underlying archival record does not, and you can reach it through any of them.

54
00:05:45.000 --> 00:05:49.000
The partners exchange public records and updates daily.

55
00:05:49.000 --> 00:05:57.650
The boxes below show the different kinds of record that describe a dataset: the reads, the sequences, the biological source, the project.

56
00:05:57.650 --> 00:06:03.025
The concepts match across the partners even though the names and the pages do not.

57
00:06:03.025 --> 00:06:07.850
Take a hypothetical study of HBB variants in beta-thalassaemia.

58
00:06:07.850 --> 00:06:15.475
The read archives would hold the raw sequencing reads, with their quality scores and a description of how the experiment was done.

59
00:06:15.475 --> 00:06:22.825
That is what lets someone else go back and inspect the evidence for a reported variant rather than taking it on trust.

60
00:06:23.425 --> 00:06:31.850
This slide is really an argument for learning principles rather than a list of database names, because archives merge and access routes change.

61
00:06:31.850 --> 00:06:37.350
The Trace Archive was set up in two thousand and one, during the big genome projects.

62
00:06:37.350 --> 00:06:48.500
It held individual sequencing reads: the chromatograms — the instrument traces, those coloured peaks — together with the sequences read off them, the quality scores, and the metadata.

63
00:06:48.500 --> 00:06:57.050
Then high-throughput sequencing arrived with far larger datasets, and NCBI released the Sequence Read Archive in two thousand and eight.

64
00:06:57.050 --> 00:06:59.725
The two coexisted for years.

65
00:06:59.725 --> 00:07:06.425
In June twenty twenty-two the separate Trace Archive was retired and its data moved into SRA.

66
00:07:06.425 --> 00:07:17.475
Japan went a different way: its trace archive closed in twenty twenty-three, and old trace data now come from SRA while new capillary data go to the Japanese read archive.

67
00:07:17.475 --> 00:07:23.000
Europe went a third way, bringing everything together inside ENA in twenty ten.

68
00:07:23.000 --> 00:07:25.475
Three routes to the same end.

69
00:07:25.475 --> 00:07:29.950
The lesson is that the data usually survive; the address does not.

70
00:07:30.550 --> 00:07:36.700
Now the question of what you actually need: genomic DNA, an RNA transcript, or a protein.

71
00:07:36.700 --> 00:07:41.100
The upper diagram is the familiar route for a protein-coding gene.

72
00:07:41.100 --> 00:07:48.600
Transcription copies DNA into RNA; translation turns the coding part of an mRNA into a polypeptide.

73
00:07:48.600 --> 00:07:55.275
Plenty of RNAs are never translated at all, so treat this as a simplified frame rather than a rule.

74
00:07:55.275 --> 00:07:58.650
The boxes below give examples at each level.

75
00:07:58.650 --> 00:08:02.950
Genomic records describe DNA and features such as genes.

76
00:08:02.950 --> 00:08:06.425
Transcript records describe RNA products.

77
00:08:06.425 --> 00:08:13.500
Protein records describe amino-acid sequences and connect them to structure, function and interactions.

78
00:08:13.500 --> 00:08:17.750
The double arrows mean these records can be navigated between.

79
00:08:17.750 --> 00:08:22.525
They do not promise that every resource provides a link in both directions.

80
00:08:23.125 --> 00:08:28.100
Four views of the same genomic DNA, each answering a different question.

81
00:08:28.100 --> 00:08:36.375
A genome assembly is a reconstruction of a genome's DNA sequence, and how complete it is depends on the data and the assembly.

82
00:08:36.375 --> 00:08:48.000
Focus on one chromosome and you get a coordinate system: numbered positions along the reference sequence, which is what lets different kinds of information be laid over each other and stay aligned.

83
00:08:48.000 --> 00:08:56.225
Enlarge a region and it may hold one gene, several genes, parts of genes, and the DNA between them — coding and non-coding both.

84
00:08:56.225 --> 00:09:02.175
Some of that non-coding DNA regulates gene activity; some has no known function.

85
00:09:02.175 --> 00:09:07.300
The wider view matters when your question is about how a gene works in its surroundings.

86
00:09:07.300 --> 00:09:15.425
Regulatory sequences control when and how strongly a gene is expressed, and some of them lie well outside the gene they control.

87
00:09:15.425 --> 00:09:18.825
A deletion can take out several genes at once.

88
00:09:18.825 --> 00:09:22.675
Look only at the coding sequence and you will miss all of it.

89
00:09:22.675 --> 00:09:25.700
HBB makes the point concretely.

90
00:09:25.700 --> 00:09:33.325
It sits in the beta-globin gene cluster on chromosome eleven, and the cluster has a control region that governs the whole set.

91
00:09:33.925 --> 00:09:40.225
At the RNA level, a transcript record describes a sequence and its relationship to a gene.

92
00:09:40.225 --> 00:09:47.600
RefSeq and Ensembl provide annotated transcripts, including the exon structure and the alternative forms.

93
00:09:47.600 --> 00:09:51.325
The three-exon picture is illustrative, not a rule.

94
00:09:51.325 --> 00:09:59.900
Because annotations mix experimental evidence, prediction and curation, check what supports the particular record you are about to use.

95
00:09:59.900 --> 00:10:10.250
Submitted transcript sequences are also in the three INSDC archives — and despite the name, the DNA Data Bank of Japan holds RNA sequences too.

96
00:10:10.250 --> 00:10:15.900
Non-coding RNAs are transcripts as well; they simply do not encode proteins.

97
00:10:15.900 --> 00:10:19.850
The tRNA in the picture is the familiar example.

98
00:10:19.850 --> 00:10:30.125
RNAcentral collects non-coding RNA sequences from specialist resources, and Rfam groups related ones into families using both sequence and structure.

99
00:10:30.125 --> 00:10:33.050
One distinction worth keeping clear.

100
00:10:33.050 --> 00:10:37.325
A sequence record tells you which RNA form you are looking at.

101
00:10:37.325 --> 00:10:42.250
Expression data tell you where it was detected and how much of it there was.

102
00:10:42.250 --> 00:10:48.200
Finding a transcript record does not establish that the transcript is present in your tissue.

103
00:10:48.800 --> 00:10:53.025
Here is why searching for one gene returns several transcripts.

104
00:10:53.025 --> 00:10:55.850
Start with a gene of three exons.

105
00:10:55.850 --> 00:11:00.200
In the baseline, splicing removes the introns and joins all three.

106
00:11:00.200 --> 00:11:06.000
Transcription can begin at different places, giving RNAs with different five-prime ends.

107
00:11:06.000 --> 00:11:12.950
Alternative splicing changes which pieces survive — here exon two is skipped while one and three keep their order.

108
00:11:12.950 --> 00:11:17.950
Alternative cleavage and polyadenylation give different three-prime ends.

109
00:11:17.950 --> 00:11:22.675
And these combine, so the picture shows possibilities rather than a count.

110
00:11:22.675 --> 00:11:26.300
Different transcripts need not give different proteins.

111
00:11:26.300 --> 00:11:32.025
A change can affect only the untranslated regions and leave the protein identical.

112
00:11:32.025 --> 00:11:37.750
So for database work, a gene name may not be enough to specify the RNA you want.

113
00:11:37.750 --> 00:11:46.825
To check whether two transcripts come from the same gene, follow their links to the gene record — transcripts of one gene point at one gene identifier.

114
00:11:46.825 --> 00:11:51.575
Compare within the same organism, database and annotation release.

115
00:11:51.575 --> 00:11:57.475
And before you settle on a sequence, check its evidence and write down its identifier and version.

116
00:11:58.075 --> 00:12:05.400
Protein records describe the amino-acid sequence and connect it to structure, function and cellular context.

117
00:12:05.400 --> 00:12:14.425
The beta-globin sequence is the kind of thing you get from UniProtKB or NCBI Protein, with annotated variants and links onwards.

118
00:12:14.425 --> 00:12:18.425
The haemoglobin picture is a three-dimensional structure.

119
00:12:18.425 --> 00:12:27.000
The Protein Data Bank is reached through three different interfaces — in the United States, Europe and Japan — but they serve one archive.

120
00:12:27.000 --> 00:12:29.800
Those models come from experiment.

121
00:12:29.800 --> 00:12:36.875
AlphaFold provides predicted models, so two similar-looking pictures can rest on entirely different evidence.

122
00:12:36.875 --> 00:12:40.175
That is worth checking before you rely on either.

123
00:12:40.175 --> 00:12:43.200
The coloured map is a domain diagram.

124
00:12:43.200 --> 00:12:51.050
Domains are recognisable structural or functional units, and Pfam and InterPro identify them and classify families.

125
00:12:51.050 --> 00:12:54.950
The network illustrates relationships between proteins.

126
00:12:54.950 --> 00:13:02.000
BioGRID and IntAct hold curated experimental interactions; STRING combines several kinds of evidence.

127
00:13:02.000 --> 00:13:07.075
A line between two proteins can mean they share a role without ever touching.

128
00:13:07.675 --> 00:13:11.050
Now, where does the content of a record come from?

129
00:13:11.050 --> 00:13:13.800
Experiments produce the measurements.

130
00:13:13.800 --> 00:13:23.850
"Raw data" means the original output kept for analysis — though instruments and their software have usually done some processing already, so raw does not mean untouched.

131
00:13:23.850 --> 00:13:31.550
Sequencing signals are physical measurements: changes in fluorescence, or in electrical current, depending on the technology.

132
00:13:31.550 --> 00:13:34.425
Software turns those into letters.

133
00:13:34.425 --> 00:13:39.375
So the familiar string of A, C, G and T is already an interpretation.

134
00:13:39.375 --> 00:13:46.875
Images record spatial information, and the measurements extracted from an image are a later stage than the image itself.

135
00:13:46.875 --> 00:13:54.375
Instrument readings — absorbance, fluorescence, mass spectra — need their units, settings and controls to mean anything.

136
00:13:54.375 --> 00:14:00.050
Phenotypic observations record what was seen, which is not the same as explaining it.

137
00:14:00.650 --> 00:14:05.825
So: we need information about a particular protein, and what we have is a name.

138
00:14:05.825 --> 00:14:13.175
Here is "human hemoglobin beta" typed into two portals — NCBI on the left, EMBL-EBI on the right.

139
00:14:13.175 --> 00:14:15.800
Both give us somewhere to start.

140
00:14:15.800 --> 00:14:20.850
To decide where to go next, we need to see how each one groups what it found.

141
00:14:20.850 --> 00:14:24.000
Start from the situation you are actually in.

142
00:14:24.000 --> 00:14:31.125
You have a name — a gene name, a protein name, something a colleague said in a corridor — and you want the data behind it.

143
00:14:31.125 --> 00:14:34.525
The difficulty is that the name is not an address.

144
00:14:34.525 --> 00:14:39.975
It is a label that several different resources have each attached to several different things.

145
00:14:39.975 --> 00:14:43.125
Both of the big portals will take that name.

146
00:14:43.125 --> 00:14:51.025
At NCBI you type it into the search box at the top of the page; at EMBL-EBI you do the same thing on their front page.

147
00:14:51.025 --> 00:14:58.100
Neither asks you what kind of record you want, which sounds helpful and is actually the first thing you will have to decide.

148
00:14:58.100 --> 00:15:02.100
So the question for the next few slides is not how to search.

149
00:15:02.100 --> 00:15:07.625
It is how to read what comes back, and how to tell which of the answers is the one you meant.

150
00:15:08.225 --> 00:15:10.350
Look at the categories.

151
00:15:10.350 --> 00:15:15.350
NCBI groups matches under Literature, Genes, Genomes, Proteins.

152
00:15:15.350 --> 00:15:21.800
EMBL-EBI lists nucleotide sequences, protein sequences, macromolecular structures.

153
00:15:21.800 --> 00:15:26.300
The same words lead to a paper, a sequence record or a structure.

154
00:15:26.300 --> 00:15:30.100
Which one is useful depends entirely on the question.

155
00:15:30.100 --> 00:15:34.625
Suppose we want the DNA around the gene that encodes beta-globin.

156
00:15:34.625 --> 00:15:36.450
Where should we look?

157
00:15:36.450 --> 00:15:41.975
This is what a name search actually returns: not a record, but a map of the collection.

158
00:15:41.975 --> 00:15:49.250
NCBI runs the query against every database it holds at once and reports how many hits each one has.

159
00:15:49.250 --> 00:15:53.175
Read that as a table of contents rather than a result.

160
00:15:53.175 --> 00:15:55.950
Nucleotide holds sequences.

161
00:15:55.950 --> 00:15:59.575
Gene holds one record per gene per organism.

162
00:15:59.575 --> 00:16:04.075
Protein holds translated and curated protein sequences.

163
00:16:04.075 --> 00:16:06.950
GEO holds expression experiments.

164
00:16:06.950 --> 00:16:09.400
PubMed holds the literature.

165
00:16:09.400 --> 00:16:17.200
The same four letters, H-B-B, mean something slightly different in each of them, and the counts differ by orders of magnitude.

166
00:16:17.200 --> 00:16:21.875
The skill being taught here is choosing the column before you open anything.

167
00:16:22.475 --> 00:16:24.425
The Genomes group.

168
00:16:24.425 --> 00:16:34.000
Nucleotide is the place to start for genomic DNA — though it holds transcript sequences too, so open the record and check what it actually describes.

169
00:16:34.000 --> 00:16:36.850
The others answer different questions.

170
00:16:36.850 --> 00:16:40.075
The read archive has the sequencing reads.

171
00:16:40.075 --> 00:16:44.300
BioProject describes the study, BioSample the material.

172
00:16:44.300 --> 00:16:51.900
That is the same separation we saw earlier: the data and their experimental context live in separate, linked records.

173
00:16:51.900 --> 00:16:56.975
Nucleotide is the largest and the least curated of the three we care about.

174
00:16:56.975 --> 00:17:03.375
A hit here is a sequence that somebody submitted, with whatever annotation they chose to attach.

175
00:17:03.375 --> 00:17:05.575
That means two things.

176
00:17:05.575 --> 00:17:13.425
First, you will see the same gene many times over — from different studies, different individuals, different lengths of flanking sequence.

177
00:17:13.425 --> 00:17:24.375
Second, the source matters more than the sequence: a record from a genome project and a record from a single PCR product can look identical in the list and mean quite different things.

178
00:17:24.375 --> 00:17:28.425
Look at the organism column and the length before anything else.

179
00:17:28.425 --> 00:17:33.825
If you cannot tell what a hit is from those two fields, open it rather than guessing.

180
00:17:34.425 --> 00:17:41.325
If the question is where the gene sits, or which transcripts belong to it, then NCBI Gene is the place.

181
00:17:41.325 --> 00:17:46.675
A gene record gives an overview and links on to the sequence records themselves.

182
00:17:46.675 --> 00:17:54.650
The same group holds GEO DataSets, which is where you would look for studies that measured expression — which tissues express this gene.

183
00:17:54.650 --> 00:17:58.450
Two resources, two different questions about one gene.

184
00:17:58.450 --> 00:18:04.125
Gene is the record type most people actually want and the one they least often search.

185
00:18:04.125 --> 00:18:15.225
There is one record per gene per organism, and it is curated: the symbol, the location, the transcripts, the phenotypes and the cross-references are all in one place and all maintained.

186
00:18:15.225 --> 00:18:19.075
The lower half of this slide is the part worth noticing.

187
00:18:19.075 --> 00:18:25.300
A gene record links straight out to expression data — which tissues, which experiments, which studies.

188
00:18:25.300 --> 00:18:32.375
That is a path from a name to an experimental result in two clicks, and it is the path we will follow on the next slide.

189
00:18:32.975 --> 00:18:37.175
At the protein level the question still decides the destination.

190
00:18:37.175 --> 00:18:40.550
Need the amino-acid sequence for an alignment?

191
00:18:40.550 --> 00:18:42.075
Protein.

192
00:18:42.075 --> 00:18:45.800
Want to look at conserved regions and family membership?

193
00:18:45.800 --> 00:18:49.425
Conserved Domains and Protein Family Models.

194
00:18:49.425 --> 00:18:53.175
Want to see how the subunits of haemoglobin fit together?

195
00:18:53.175 --> 00:18:54.350
Structure.

196
00:18:54.350 --> 00:18:58.925
Choosing the protein level narrows what kind of information you are after.

197
00:18:58.925 --> 00:19:02.275
You still have to choose the collection that holds it.

198
00:19:02.275 --> 00:19:08.475
A protein name is the most ambiguous thing you can type into a search box, and this slide shows why.

199
00:19:08.475 --> 00:19:20.400
The same protein has a RefSeq record, a UniProt record, a structure in the PDB, domain assignments in Pfam and InterPro, and an entry in whatever interaction resource you prefer.

200
00:19:20.400 --> 00:19:23.025
They are not duplicates of each other.

201
00:19:23.025 --> 00:19:28.425
RefSeq gives you the sequence that NCBI curates alongside its mRNA.

202
00:19:28.425 --> 00:19:33.550
UniProt gives you the function, the evidence for that function and the variants.

203
00:19:33.550 --> 00:19:36.400
The PDB gives you coordinates.

204
00:19:36.400 --> 00:19:43.325
Which one you want depends entirely on the question, and the question is usually the thing that has not been stated clearly.

205
00:19:43.925 --> 00:19:47.000
The same approach works at EMBL-EBI.

206
00:19:47.000 --> 00:19:52.475
The category list on the left narrows to sequences, structures or expression data.

207
00:19:52.475 --> 00:20:00.925
On the right, the protein results include both the beta and the alpha subunits — so check the protein name and the organism before choosing.

208
00:20:00.925 --> 00:20:05.225
Three checks, every time, before you take a result as the answer.

209
00:20:05.225 --> 00:20:07.025
The organism.

210
00:20:07.025 --> 00:20:16.475
A search for a gene name will cheerfully return the mouse record above the human one, because relevance is computed from the text and not from what you had in mind.

211
00:20:16.475 --> 00:20:18.600
The review status.

212
00:20:18.600 --> 00:20:22.950
In UniProt, reviewed means a curator has read the evidence.

213
00:20:22.950 --> 00:20:26.075
Unreviewed means a pipeline produced it.

214
00:20:26.075 --> 00:20:29.225
Both are useful; they are not interchangeable.

215
00:20:29.225 --> 00:20:32.025
And whether the record is a reference.

216
00:20:32.025 --> 00:20:39.550
One organism has one reference genome and one reference transcript per gene; everything else is an alternative.

217
00:20:39.550 --> 00:20:44.900
If your figure legend will eventually name an accession, it should usually be that one.

218
00:20:45.500 --> 00:20:48.750
The broad search showed several kinds of result.

219
00:20:48.750 --> 00:20:52.775
Now suppose the question is specifically about the human gene.

220
00:20:52.775 --> 00:20:56.600
Select Gene at NCBI and use the same words.

221
00:20:56.600 --> 00:21:04.125
Both screenshots show four hundred and nineteen gene hits, which brings us into that collection to look at individual records.

222
00:21:04.125 --> 00:21:07.450
From here we do it properly rather than quickly.

223
00:21:07.450 --> 00:21:13.975
Instead of searching everything at once, choose the resource first — Gene, in this case — and search inside it.

224
00:21:13.975 --> 00:21:20.625
The effect is immediate: the result list is one kind of thing, and the columns mean the same thing in every row.

225
00:21:20.625 --> 00:21:28.750
Almost every complaint about biological database search comes down to having searched the whole site when a single resource was meant.

226
00:21:29.350 --> 00:21:34.050
The first row is haemoglobin subunit beta in Homo sapiens.

227
00:21:34.050 --> 00:21:38.700
Further down there is a human haemoglobin subunit gamma record too.

228
00:21:38.700 --> 00:21:42.400
So the organism alone does not tell us which to open.

229
00:21:42.400 --> 00:21:47.600
We read the description as well, and then open the record for the beta subunit.

230
00:21:47.600 --> 00:21:53.375
The record opens on the name and the organism, and that is where to look first, not last.

231
00:21:53.375 --> 00:21:58.425
Hemoglobin subunit beta, Homo sapiens, gene symbol HBB.

232
00:21:58.425 --> 00:22:01.300
Check the symbol against what you typed.

233
00:22:01.300 --> 00:22:12.700
Gene symbols are reused across species and reassigned over time; the approved symbol in the record is the authority, and anything else you have written down is a synonym until proved otherwise.

234
00:22:13.300 --> 00:22:17.000
The summary confirms which gene and which organism.

235
00:22:17.000 --> 00:22:20.075
The sections below take different questions.

236
00:22:20.075 --> 00:22:23.425
Genomic context shows where the gene lies.

237
00:22:23.425 --> 00:22:28.425
Genomic regions, transcripts and products leads to the sequence records.

238
00:22:28.425 --> 00:22:34.150
And if the question is how much RNA was detected in different tissues, there is Expression.

239
00:22:34.150 --> 00:22:40.100
The record itself is long, and it is worth knowing its shape rather than reading it top to bottom.

240
00:22:40.100 --> 00:22:44.400
Genomic context tells you where the gene sits and what is next to it.

241
00:22:44.400 --> 00:22:49.200
The transcript section lists every annotated transcript with its protein.

242
00:22:49.200 --> 00:22:52.700
Phenotypes links out to the clinical resources.

243
00:22:52.700 --> 00:22:57.950
Related sequences is the full list of accessions associated with this gene.

244
00:22:57.950 --> 00:23:02.625
Cross-references point at every other resource that holds something about it.

245
00:23:02.625 --> 00:23:05.225
You are rarely reading the record.

246
00:23:05.225 --> 00:23:09.325
You are navigating it to a particular section and then leaving.

